# Docs standards

Agents read `CLAUDE.md`, the rules, the agent files, `docs/phases/STATUS.md`, the active phase file and the sections of `docs/brief.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/design-system.md` and `docs/design-system-table.md` their task touches before they work. They find those sections through `docs/INDEX.md` and read one with `pnpm docs:section <file> "<heading>"`. Every line there costs context on every run. These docs say what is true now. History is in git: a commit message says what changed and why, and `git log -- <file>` finds it. A reviewer reports a breach as a finding.

## Current state, not history

- When a change supersedes a rule, a design or a plan step, rewrite the passage so it states the new rule. Do not append "was / now", "replaces phase N", "previously", or a dated note under the old text.
- No dated "built" tags on headings ("— built (2026-10-27)"). A thing that is not built says "Not built." in one line.
- Keep an authority stamp on a rule the user set, short: "(user instruction, 2026-10-27)". It cites who decided, not how it was reached.
- Say each rule once, in the doc that owns it (`.cursor/rules/authority.mdc` gives the owners), and point to it from elsewhere. Do not copy a rule into an agent file, a phase file or a plan.

## Facts the code owns

- Do not write down a number or a list the code holds: the engine version, how many scenarios are playable, how many lines are open, the families, branch names, test counts. Point to the file (`src/rules/engine/version.ts`, `src/campaign/playable.ts`, `src/effects/open-lines.ts`, `src/catalog/families/index.ts`).
- Cite a doc by file and section heading, never by line number: lines move with every edit. Write the heading's words (`docs/brief.md, Core round`); `scripts/docs/index.test.ts` fails when a citation in code or a live doc names no heading, so rename a heading and its citations together.

## The index

- `docs/INDEX.md` is generated by `pnpm docs:index` and is the only doc with line numbers. Do not edit it by hand. Run the command after adding, removing or renaming a doc or a heading, or after any doc edit that moves lines; the test says when it is stale.
- A new doc agents should read goes in a folder the index lists (`scripts/docs/index.ts`, `groups`). A long doc keeps `##`/`###` headings for each topic, so the index can point into it: a bold run-in label is not a heading.
- When one section grows past a few thousand tokens and only some tasks need it, split it into its own file (as `docs/design-system-table.md` was) and leave a one-line pointer where it was.

## `docs/phases/STATUS.md`

- The rules header, then one row per phase. A note is at most about 200 characters: the date, and only what is still open (`engine done`, a review not run, phone checks, an unrun flow, deferred work).
- **Open items**: one line per thing still to do, grouped by area. Delete the line when it is done. Do not move it to a "done" list.
- **Standing decisions**: one line per user decision that still constrains later work, with its date. Delete it when the work it constrained is finished and nothing later depends on it.
- No dated sections, no "Built / Decided / Checked" blocks, no fixed review findings, test counts, timings or commit hashes. Those go in the commit message and the final report to the user.

## Plans, phases and handovers

- A plan under `docs/plans/` is live while its work is open. When the work is done, move it to `docs/plans/done/` and update the paths that cite it. Agents do not read `done/` unless a task names it.
- A phase file is its spec and exit. When the phase is completed, delete the file; git keeps it. Its open items stay in `docs/phases/STATUS.md`, and a citation of it in code or a live doc points to the doc that owns the behaviour (`docs/brief.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, the design system) instead.
- A handover in `docs/handover/` exists while work is in flight or open. The final commit of that work deletes it, or cuts it to the points still open. Device checks that are all that is left go to `docs/phases/STATUS.md`, Phone and tablet checks, and the handover is deleted.
- A paste-once prompt for a new session is not committed. Give it to the user in the report, or put it in the scratchpad.
